13:07:10 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That's a really intuitive comparison, but it breaks down because of one crucial difference: **the assembly-line robot arm is a complete, self-contained system, while a bionic limb is only part of a system that includes a human body.** Here's the key distinction: **The robot arm:** - Bolted to a fixed base - Powered directly by an electrical outlet (unlimited power) - Controlled by a computer program that doesn't get tired, distracted, or need coordination with anything else **The bionic limb:** - Attached to a residual limb made of the amputee's actual muscle, skin, bone, and nerves - Powered by a battery that must be *carried* as extra weight - Controlled by *biological* signals — either residual muscle contractions (myoelectric control) or nerve signals — which absolutely do fatigue like any other muscle activation - Must be stabilized and coordinated by the rest of the person's body (core, shoulder, back, opposite limb) So even though the actuators, motors, and joints in the prosthesis itself don't get "sore" the way muscle does, several very human sources of fatigue remain: 1. **Muscular fatigue in the residual limb** — myoelectric prosthetics require sustained, precise muscle contractions to generate control signals, which tires those muscles just like any other muscle use. 2. **Mechanical load fatigue** — the prosthesis has mass. Carrying and swinging that mass, especially for upper-limb devices, requires shoulder/torso effort that adds up over a day. 3. **Skin and soft-tissue fatigue** — the socket interface creates friction and pressure; skin and tissue can become irritated or sore with prolonged use. 4. **Compensatory whole-body fatigue** — other muscles work overtime to stabilize, balance, or compensate for a joint that doesn't have full proprioceptive feedback. 5. **Cognitive/attentional fatigue** — controlling the device (especially early on) requires conscious effort and concentration, which is mentally tiring in a way a factory robot never experiences. So the "robot part" of the bionic limb doesn't get tired—you're right about that. But the human part that has to power, wear, and operate it very much does. It's less like comparing a human worker to an independent robot, and more like asking whether wearing a exoskeleton or backpack reduces fatigue: the tool can help, but you're still the one supplying the energy and control.
